-- D6 state foundation, tier C: closes item #4 from the pre-deploy gap
-- list -- "possible minority-elected State Board of Education states not
-- caught by migration 072's 'all or majority elected' framing." That
-- framing undersold the real gap: this migration adds 5 MORE
-- jurisdictions with genuinely elected board seats that a "majority
-- elected" search summary simply doesn't surface, found by going to a
-- primary authoritative source instead of continuing to search
-- piecemeal -- the Education Commission of the States' full 50-state K-12
-- governance comparison (reports.ecs.org/comparisons/k-12-governance-
-- state-board-of-education-03), which gives an exhaustive per-state
-- selection-method breakdown in one place. Every state ECS flagged as
-- having ANY elected component was then individually cross-checked
-- against that state's own Ballotpedia "Selection Method" field before
-- building anything -- same discipline as every other migration in this
-- project, not taken on ECS's word alone.
--
-- New jurisdictions added: Louisiana's Board of Elementary and Secondary
-- Education (8 elected districts + 3 governor-appointed at-large seats,
-- excluded), Ohio's State Board of Education (11 elected districts + 8
-- governor-appointed at-large seats, one currently vacant, excluded),
-- Nevada's State Board of Education (4 elected by congressional
-- district + 3 seats appointed by the Governor/Senate Majority Leader/
-- Assembly Speaker respectively, excluded), New Mexico's Public
-- Education Commission (10 constitutional seats, 9 currently filled, a
-- real vacancy not guessed -- confirmed still active: a 2024 ballot
-- measure to replace it with a department was not adopted), and the
-- District of Columbia's State Board of Education (9 seats, ALL
-- elected -- the only jurisdiction in this migration with no appointed
-- component at all).
--
-- Two more states ECS flagged with SOME elected component were checked
-- and deliberately EXCLUDED, same discipline already applied throughout
-- this project to legislature- or official-selected (not popularly
-- elected) offices: South Carolina's district members are elected BY
-- LEGISLATIVE DELEGATIONS, not by voters. Washington's members are
-- elected by school district directors and by the boards of state-
-- approved private schools -- also indirect election by officials, not
-- the general public (Washington's Superintendent of Public Instruction,
-- which IS genuinely popularly elected, is already modeled separately in
-- migration 065). New York's board is selected by the legislature
-- outright, same exclusion.
--
-- District of Columbia is modeled at the 'county' level in this schema
-- (jurisdiction_id ocd-division/country:us/district:dc), matching its
-- existing Attorney General office from migration 064, not 'state' --
-- every other office in this migration uses 'state'.
--
-- This should now be the definitive, exhaustively-sourced answer to item
-- #4 -- 12 total jurisdictions with genuinely popularly-elected State
-- Board of Education seats: the 7 from migration 072 (Alabama, Colorado,
-- Kansas, Michigan, Nebraska, Texas, Utah) plus the 5 here (Louisiana,
-- Ohio, Nevada, New Mexico, District of Columbia).



